    <dcterms:title>The Rolliad, in Two Parts
Probationary Odes for the Laureatship &amp; Political Eclogues</dcterms:title>
    <dcterms:alternative>The Rolliad, in Two Parts&#13;
Probationary Odes for the Laureatship and Political Eclogues</dcterms:alternative>
    <dcterms:description>Wikipedia page about this book: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolliad</dcterms:description>
    <pgterms:marc508>Produced by Steffen Haugk</pgterms:marc508>
    <pgterms:marc520>"The Rolliad, in Two Parts" by Tickell, Ellis, Laurence, and Richardson is a work of British political satire published serially in 1784–85. This collaborative work takes the ingenious form of literary criticism examining a fictional epic poem about John Rolle, MP for Devon, being guided through Parliament by Merlin. The satire primarily targets Prime Minister William Pitt the Younger and his administration, mocking Pitt's youth, personal habits, and political allies through witty verse and biting commentary that captured the political tensions of its era. (This is an automatically generated summary.)</pgterms:marc520>
    <pgterms:marc908>Reading ease score: 64.6 (8th &amp; 9th grade). Neither easy nor difficult to read.</pgterms:marc908>
